<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burgundy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Burgundian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burgundy is a historical region of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the name of a powerful late medieval duchy whose lands also included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. Through the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian of Habsburg</a>, the Burgundian inheritance passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. It formed part of the territories inherited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, contributing to the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>'s European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burgundy.jpg" alt="Burgundy" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patricia.fidi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bourgogne.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun (843 CE) formalised a threefold division into West Francia under Charles the Bald, Middle Francia (from the Low Countries through <b>Burgundy</b> to Italy) under Lothair, and East Francia under Louis the German.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the dukes of <b>Burgundy</b>, Flanders became the core of a rich new state, and its painters, the Flemish Primitives, transformed European art; Jan van Eyck worked in Bruges, and the Ghent Altarpiece was completed in 1432.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hainaut passed through several dynasties: the house of Flanders, then the Avesnes, who also held Holland and Zeeland from 1299, then the Wittelsbachs from 1345 and the Valois dukes of <b>Burgundy</b> from 1433, before the Habsburgs inherited it in 1482.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 893 William the Pious, count of Auvergne, began to style himself duke of the Aquitanians, the first prince to hold the title in this new sense, and in 910 he founded the abbey of Cluny in <b>Burgundy</b>, whose monastic reform would spread across Latin Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1395 Duke Philip the Bold ordered the Gamay grape uprooted from <b>Burgundy</b>’s vineyards, calling it a ‘very bad and disloyal’ plant, in favour of Pinot noir.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy’s greatest military moment came under René II, whose lands lay across the corridor Charles the Bold of <b>Burgundy</b> needed to link his territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Military</a></li></ul></section>
